{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EJxRJskMiX6sYwiAK4yFo4","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CoRL 2024 review form overall score scale 1-10 accept reject site:2024.corl.org OR site:openreview.net\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CoRL 2024 - Instruction for Reviews\",\"url\":\"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2024 Conference | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL%2F2024%2FConference\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2024\",\"url\":\"https://2024.corl.org/\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2024 | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org/CoRL/2024\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2024 Conference Submissions | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/submissions?venue=robot-learning.org/CoRL/2024/Conference\"},{\"title\":\"Default Review Form | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://docs.openreview.net/reference/default-forms/default-review-form\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2024 Workshop XE | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL%2F2024%2FWorkshop%2FXE\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2024 - Instruction for Authors\",\"url\":\"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-authors\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL\"},{\"title\":\"CoRL 2024 - Papers XX\",\"url\":\"https://2024.corl.org/program/papers-xx\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the CoRL 2024 review form scoring scale. The overall rating scale uses a 1-10 score with the following categories: \"10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\", \"9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\", \"8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\", \"7: Good paper, accept\", \"6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\", \"5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\", \"4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\", \"3: Clear rejection\", \"2: Strong rejection\", \"1: Trivial or wrong\"\n\nThis scoring system is used in the OpenReview platform where CoRL 2024 submissions were managed. Scores 7-10 are generally associated with accept recommendations, while scores 4-5 fall around the acceptance threshold, and scores 1-3 indicate rejection.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d09a97eb-2874-493a-b7bd-2546bcb68a45","uuid":"ddd83f07-aea8-472c-85c4-3c4840bdb2bb","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:09:52.615Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CoRL 2024 review form overall score scale 1-10 accept reject site:2024.corl.org OR site:openreview.net","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_017UQD2GvabhtS9LnbcJug31","content":[{"title":"CoRL 2024 - Instruction for Reviews","url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"},{"title":"CoRL 2024 Conference | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL%2F2024%2FConference"},{"title":"CoRL 2024","url":"https://2024.corl.org/"},{"title":"CoRL 2024 | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org/CoRL/2024"},{"title":"CoRL 2024 Conference Submissions | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/submissions?venue=robot-learning.org/CoRL/2024/Conference"},{"title":"Default Review Form | OpenReview","url":"https://docs.openreview.net/reference/default-forms/default-review-form"},{"title":"CoRL 2024 Workshop XE | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL%2F2024%2FWorkshop%2FXE"},{"title":"CoRL 2024 - Instruction for Authors","url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-authors"},{"title":"CoRL | OpenReview","url":"https://openreview.net/group?id=robot-learning.org%2FCoRL"},{"title":"CoRL 2024 - Papers XX","url":"https://2024.corl.org/program/papers-xx"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about the CoRL 2024 review form scoring scale. The overall rating scale uses a 1-10 score with the following categories: \"10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\", \"9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\", \"8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\", \"7: Good paper, accept\", \"6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\", \"5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\", \"4: Ok but not good enough - rejection\", \"3: Clear rejection\", \"2: Strong rejection\", \"1: Trivial or wrong\"\n\nThis scoring system is used in the OpenReview platform where CoRL 2024 submissions were managed. Scores 7-10 are generally associated with accept recommendations, while scores 4-5 fall around the acceptance threshold, and scores 1-3 indicate rejection."],"durationSeconds":4.219555652999996}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011oQT8Empm4zxtQHvY6wRuT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me summarize:\n\n**CoRL Review Form (based on CoRL 2024):**\n- Overall score: 1-10 scale\n  - 10: Top 5%, seminal paper\n  - 9: Top 15%, strong accept\n  - 8: Top 50%, clear accept\n  - 7: Good paper, accept\n  - 6: Marginally above threshold\n  - 5: Marginally below threshold\n  - 4: Ok but not good enough, rejection\n  - 3: Clear rejection\n  - 2: Strong rejection\n  - 1: Trivial or wrong\n- CoRL acceptance rate ~30%\n- Key dimensions: significance, novelty, relevance to robot learning, quality of experiments, clarity\n\n**Paper Summary:**\n\"Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\nThe paper claims that frozen VLA activations can encode perturbation types with perfect F1 scores at layer 6, and uses this to route to specialized LoRA experts for handling different types of distribution shifts.\n\nThe results show substantial improvements on LIBERO-Plus—78.9% versus 65.1% for a parameter-matched baseline, a 13.8 percentage point gain. Importantly, random routing only improves by 0.7 points, which rules out simple ensemble effects, and the oracle gap is just 1.8 points, suggesting the routing mechanism is nearly optimal. The approach generalizes across architectures too, with SmolVLA showing a consistent 14.2 point improvement, while performance on clean LIBERO remains essentially flat.\n\nThe review context includes detailed experimental plans covering expert training, the Meta-Brain assembly process, oracle routing ablations, ablation studies, reproduction of related work, parameter efficiency analysis, and router architecture variations.\n\nNow I'm laying out the full review process: I'll dispatch two parallel reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, another on novelty and related work—then run a structured roundtable with independent assessments, cross-sharing of perspectives, convergence on key points, and finally a synthesis of findings.\n\nFor the CoRL evaluation, I need to establish the scoring framework: 1-10 scale with dimensions covering significance, novelty, robot learning relevance, soundness, clarity, and reproducibility, keeping in mind that ~30% acceptance means scores of 7+ typically get accepted.\n\nLet me construct the reviewer prompts carefully and get both agents working in parallel.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":759,"cache_read_input_tokens":64399,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":759},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d09a97eb-2874-493a-b7bd-2546bcb68a45","uuid":"2b0f6081-1db1-4162-ab55-dcface68a394"}
