{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0166zDQyMPoXxazbc9LJLYDs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\texperiments:\n2\t- id: plan_001\n3\t  claim: 1\n4\t  title: Phase 0 — Perturbation classification from frozen activations\n5\t  description: 'Train 2-layer MLP on frozen pi0 PaliGemma activations for 3-class\n6\t    perturbation classification (Visual/Spatial/Physical). Sweep layers, pooling strategies,\n7\t    learning rates. Includes binary sanity check, 6-class comparison, few-shot K=1/3/5/10\n8\t    analysis.\n9\t\n10\t    '\n11\t  priority: P0\n12\t  status: done\n13\t  registry_id: phase0_activation_collection\n14\t  gpu_budget_hours: 5\n15\t  deps: []\n16\t  key_results: 3-class F1=0.993, binary AUC=1.0, K=1 F1=0.898, layer6 K=1=1.0\n17\t- id: plan_002\n18\t  claim: 2\n19\t  title: LoRA expert training — 3 perturbation-specific + 1 universal baseline\n20\t  description: \"Train 4 LoRA adapters on frozen pi0 PaliGemma attention layers (rank\\\n21\t    \\ 16, Q/K/V/O projections): (a) LoRA-V: trained on Visual perturbation rollouts\\\n22\t    \\ (camera+light+background+noise) (b) LoRA-S: trained on Spatial perturbation\\\n23\t    \\ rollouts (layout) (c) LoRA-P: trained on Physical perturbation rollouts (robot)\\\n24\t    \\ (d) Universal LoRA: trained on mixed all-perturbation rollouts Each on 1 GPU\\\n25\t    \\ in parallel (4 GPUs total).\\nTraining data strategy (two-pronged): (a) Visual\\\n26\t    \\ perturbations (LoRA-V): Use LIBERO standard expert demonstrations with ONLINE\\n\\\n27\t    \\    visual augmentation during training (camera jitter, brightness/contrast shifts,\\n\\\n28\t    \\    background texture swap, Gaussian noise). Action labels are unchanged because\\\n29\t    \\ visual\\n    perturbations don't alter the physical task. This is analogous to\\\n30\t    \\ FTM's training setup.\\n(b) Spatial/Physical perturbations (LoRA-S, LoRA-P):\\\n31\t    \\ Collect expert demos via frozen pi0 rollouts in perturbed environments +\\\n32\t    \\ success filtering. LIBERO has no scripted oracle policy.\\n    Physical: BDDL init_state index 1-49 (50 robot initial states), ~400 rollouts → ~150-200 successes.\\n    Spatial: MUST use LIBERO-Plus official _add_N perturbation (adds distractor objects) to match\\\n33\t    \\ eval distribution. If LIBERO-Plus assets unavailable, eval must also switch to perturb_region_info().\\n    Target: ~200 successful demos per category.\\n(c) Universal LoRA: Mix of\\\n34\t    \\ all above data sources. Training objective: SFT (behavior cloning) — minimize\\\n35\t    \\ flow-matching action prediction loss on (perturbed_observation, correct_action)\\\n36\t    \\ pairs. Match pi0's action head format. Convergence criterion: validation action\\\n37\t    \\ MSE plateaus for 3 consecutive eval checkpoints. LoRA config: rank 16 on Q/K/V/O\\\n38\t    \\ of ALL PaliGemma attention layers (18 layers). All 3 experts use identical layer\\\n39\t    \\ coverage initially (per Reviewer feedback — Phase 0 shows <2.5pp spread across\\\n40\t    \\ layers, no evidence for layer-specific assignment). Layer placement ablation\\\n41\t    \\ deferred to plan_012.\\nEvaluate each expert on its own perturbation type AND\\\n42\t    \\ cross-type (mismatched expert).\\n\"\n43\t  priority: P0\n44\t  gpu_budget_hours: 55\n45\t  deps: []\n46\t  metrics:\n47\t  - per_expert_success_rate_on_own_type\n48\t  - per_expert_success_rate_cross_type\n49\t  - universal_lora_success_rate_all_types\n50\t  registry_id: phase1_lora_universal_r16\n51\t- id: plan_003\n52\t  claim: 2\n53\t  title: Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank end-to-end\n54\t  description: 'Assemble PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, 2-layer MLP) + 3 LoRA experts\n55\t    with soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + sum_k p(type_k) * LoRA_k(h_frozen).\n56\t    End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters (only tune PAR routing\n57\t    weights). Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark (all 7 perturbation types × all\n58\t    task suites).\n59\t\n60\t    '\n61\t  priority: P0\n62\t  gpu_budget_hours: 15\n63\t  deps:\n64\t  - plan_002\n65\t  metrics:\n66\t  - libero_plus_overall_success_rate\n67\t  - per_perturbation_success_rate_breakdown\n68\t  - routing_weight_distribution_per_type\n69\t- id: plan_004\n70\t  claim: 2\n71\t  title: Oracle routing ablation — upper bound for routing quality\n72\t  description: 'Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (oracle\n73\t    routing). Measures the gap between learned routing and perfect routing. If gap\n74\t    is small, PAR is sufficient; if large, router needs improvement.\n75\t\n76\t    '\n77\t  priority: P1\n78\t  gpu_budget_hours: 5\n79\t  deps:\n80\t  - plan_002\n81\t  metrics:\n82\t  - oracle_vs_par_success_rate_gap\n83\t- id: plan_005\n84\t  claim: 2\n85\t  title: Ablation ladder — Frozen → Universal LoRA → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle\n86\t  description: \"Systematic comparison on LIBERO-Plus with 5 seeds: (a) Frozen pi0\\\n87\t    \\ baseline (no adaptation) (b) Universal LoRA (rank 16, ~3.7M params) (b2) Universal\\\n88\t    \\ LoRA (rank 48, ~12M params — PARAMETER-MATCHED to Meta-Brain total.\\n     THIS\\\n89\t    \\ IS THE EXISTENTIAL CONTROL: if rank-48 universal ≈ Meta-Brain, routing\\n   \\\n90\t    \\  adds no value. Dream reviewers flagged this as the #1 critical missing experiment.)\\n\\\n91\t    (c) Random routing (randomly assign one of 3 LoRA experts per episode — controls\\n\\\n92\t    \\    for ensemble effect. If random ≈ Meta-Brain, Claim 2 is falsified)\\n(d) Meta-Brain\\\n93\t    \\ (PAR + 3 LoRA experts, ~12M params) (e) Oracle routing (ground-truth type →\\\n94\t    \\ LoRA expert) Report overall success rate + per-perturbation breakdown. This\\\n95\t    \\ is the core evidence for Claim 2. The critical comparison is (c) vs (d): Meta-Brain\\\n96\t    \\ must significantly outperform random routing to prove routing matters.\\n\"\n97\t  priority: P0\n98\t  gpu_budget_hours: 25\n99\t  deps:\n100\t  - plan_003\n101\t  - plan_004\n102\t  metrics:\n103\t  - ablation_ladder_table\n104\t  - gain_over_frozen_per_method\n105\t  - gain_over_universal_lora\n106\t  - meta_brain_vs_random_routing_gap\n107\t- id: plan_006\n108\t  claim: 3\n109\t  title: FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline comparison\n110\t  description: 'Reproduce FTM on pi0 for visual perturbation types (camera, light,\n111\t    background, noise). Compare Meta-Brain LoRA-V vs FTM on visual perturbations.\n112\t    Meta-Brain must show value beyond FTM by additionally handling spatial/physical.\n113\t\n114\t    '\n115\t  priority: P1\n116\t  gpu_budget_hours: 10\n117\t  deps: []\n118\t  metrics:\n119\t  - ftm_vs_lora_v_on_visual_perturbations\n120\t  - meta_brain_advantage_on_non_visual\n121\t- id: plan_007\n122\t  claim: 3\n123\t  title: Parameter efficiency analysis — gain-over-baseline vs trainable params\n124\t  description: 'Compute gain-over-frozen-baseline per method and normalize by trainable\n125\t    parameters. Plot scatter: x=trainable params (log scale), y=gain-over-baseline.\n126\t    Include: Meta-Brain (12M), Universal LoRA (3.7M), FTM (4K-4.7M), VLA-GSE (114M,\n127\t    from paper). This is the visual evidence for Claim 3.\n128\t\n129\t    '\n130\t  priority: P0\n131\t  gpu_budget_hours: 0\n132\t  deps:\n133\t  - plan_005\n134\t  - plan_006\n135\t  metrics:\n136\t  - parameter_efficiency_scatter_plot\n137\t  - gain_per_million_params\n138\t- id: plan_008\n139\t  claim: 2\n140\t  title: Correction Memory ablation — with/without CM\n141\t  description: 'Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM adds\n142\t    routing-weight modulation from retrieved exemplars. Quantify marginal gain of\n143\t    CM on overall and per-perturbation success.\n144\t\n145\t    '\n146\t  priority: P1\n147\t  gpu_budget_hours: 10\n148\t  deps:\n149\t  - plan_003\n150\t  metrics:\n151\t  - cm_ablation_delta\n152\t  - cm_benefit_per_perturbation_type\n153\t- id: plan_009\n154\t  claim: 1\n155\t  title: Router architecture comparison — MLP vs linear vs attention\n156\t  description: 'Compare PAR variants: (a) 2-layer MLP (default), (b) linear classifier,\n157\t    (c) attention-based router. All on PaliGemma layer 6. Measures whether routing\n158\t    accuracy changes translate to downstream task success.\n159\t\n160\t    '\n161\t  priority: P2\n162\t  gpu_budget_hours: 5\n163\t  deps:\n164\t  - plan_002\n165\t  metrics:\n166\t  - router_accuracy_comparison\n167\t  - downstream_success_by_router_type\n168\t- id: plan_010\n169\t  claim: 2\n170\t  title: 6-class vs 3-class routing comparison\n171\t  description: 'Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot)\n172\t    with 6 LoRA experts vs 3-class coarse routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical) with 3\n173\t    experts. Tests whether finer-grained routing justifies additional parameters.\n174\t\n175\t    '\n176\t  priority: P2\n177\t  gpu_budget_hours: 15\n178\t  deps:\n179\t  - plan_002\n180\t  metrics:\n181\t  - 6class_vs_3class_overall_success\n182\t  - marginal_gain_per_additional_expert\n183\t- id: plan_013\n184\t  claim: 2\n185\t  title: Rank-48 parameter-matched universal LoRA — existential control\n186\t  description: 'Train a single universal LoRA with rank 48 (~12M params, matching\n187\t    Meta-Brain''s total parameter budget) on mixed all-perturbation data. This is\n188\t    the #1 critical experiment flagged by Dream reviewers across ALL three tiers:\n189\t    - If rank-48 universal ≈ Meta-Brain → routing adds zero value, Claim 2 collapses\n190\t    - If rank-48 universal < Meta-Brain → routing genuinely helps beyond parameter\n191\t    scaling Run alongside plan_002 on same GPU as Universal rank-16.\n192\t\n193\t    '\n194\t  priority: P0\n195\t  gpu_budget_hours: 15\n196\t  deps: []\n197\t  metrics:\n198\t  - rank48_universal_vs_meta_brain_gap\n199\t  - claim2_survival_test\n200\t- id: plan_014\n201\t  claim: 2\n202\t  title: LoRA rank sweep (r=16/32/64) — capacity bottleneck test\n203\t  description: 'Test whether routing benefit grows with expert capacity. Train LoRA-V\n204\t    at rank 16/32/64 and evaluate with/without routing at each rank. Dream worst-case\n205\t    flagged: if routing benefit emerges at higher rank, the narrative shifts to \"routing\n206\t    requires minimum expert capacity C*\" — a concrete, actionable finding. Even 1\n207\t    datapoint at rank 32 is valuable.\n208\t\n209\t    '\n210\t  priority: P1\n211\t  gpu_budget_hours: 20\n212\t  deps:\n213\t  - plan_002\n214\t  metrics:\n215\t  - routing_benefit_vs_rank_curve\n216\t  - capacity_threshold_for_routing\n217\t- id: plan_015\n218\t  claim: 2\n219\t  title: Clean LIBERO evaluation — nominal performance preservation\n220\t  description: 'Evaluate Meta-Brain on standard (unperturbed) LIBERO to verify routing\n221\t    does not degrade nominal performance. Dream reviewers flagged this as important\n222\t    for practical deployment story.\n223\t\n224\t    '\n225\t  priority: P1\n226\t  gpu_budget_hours: 5\n227\t  deps:\n228\t  - plan_003\n229\t  metrics:\n230\t  - nominal_libero_success_with_routing\n231\t  - performance_delta_vs_frozen\n232\t- id: plan_011\n233\t  claim: 3\n234\t  title: Cross-backbone validation — SmolVLA or OpenVLA\n235\t  description: 'Replicate Phase 0 + Phase 1 core pipeline on a second VLA backbone\n236\t    (SmolVLA or OpenVLA) to test generalizability of the perturbation-diagnostic routing\n237\t    principle across architectures.\n238\t\n239\t    '\n240\t  priority: P2\n241\t  gpu_budget_hours: 30\n242\t  deps:\n243\t  - plan_005\n244\t  metrics:\n245\t  - cross_backbone_phase0_f1\n246\t  - cross_backbone_gain_over_baseline\n247\t- id: plan_012\n248\t  claim: 2\n249\t  title: LoRA layer placement ablation\n250\t  description: 'Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA experts: (a) early layers\n251\t    only (2-6), (b) mid layers (6-12), (c) late layers (12-16), (d) all layers. Test\n252\t    hypothesis that Visual LoRA benefits from early layers, Spatial from mid, Physical\n253\t    from late.\n254\t\n255\t    '\n256\t  priority: P2\n257\t  gpu_budget_hours: 15\n258\t  deps:\n259\t  - plan_002\n260\t  metrics:\n261\t  - optimal_layer_placement_per_expert_type\n262\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016xH4KfuHaHHSJe4YFCJzHk","session_id":"d09a97eb-2874-493a-b7bd-2546bcb68a45","uuid":"f77db8de-94a8-47d4-bccf-7067af0a03fc","timestamp":"2026-05-15T18:11:29.724Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011oQT8Empm4zxtQHvY6wRuT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LsBEg2hEM2UhXf8DbuTHmK","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Score scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 9=top 15% strong accept, 8=top 50% clear accept, 7=good accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below threshold, 4=rejection, 3=clear rejection, 2=strong rejection, 1=trivial/wrong)\n- Acceptance rate ~30%. Papers must be relevant to robot learning.\n\n## Paper: \"Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n### Summary\nMeta-Brain diagnoses perturbation type from frozen VLA activations and routes to type-specialized LoRA experts. Key empirical finding: frozen PaliGemma layer 6 first-token activations achieve F1=1.0 on 3-class perturbation classification (visual/spatial/physical). A PAR router (2-layer MLP, 0.53M params) produces soft routing weights over 3 LoRA experts (LoRA-V, LoRA-S, LoRA-P, each rank-16, ~3.7M params). On LIBERO-Plus: Meta-Brain 78.9% vs parameter-matched universal LoRA r48 65.1% (+13.8pp, p<0.001). Random routing 65.8% (+0.7pp n.s.). Oracle gap 1.8pp. Cross-backbone on SmolVLA: +14.2pp. Clean LIBERO preserved.\n\n### Related Work Section (Section 2)\nThe paper covers 4 areas:\n1. **VLA Foundations**: pi0 [1], OpenVLA [2], SmolVLA [3], Octo [4], RT-2 [5]. Claims shared vulnerability to environmental perturbations.\n2. **VLA Robustness**: VLA-GSE [8] (MoE gating, 114M params, 81.2% on LIBERO-Plus with Qwen3-VL-4B), SAFE [9] (detection only, no correction), RobustVLA [10] (adversarial training), FTM [11] and FLA [12] (visual-only), SRPO [13] and CorridorVLA [14] (full model training). Claims \"None combines perturbation diagnosis with type-specific correction routing.\"\n3. **MoE Routing**: Switch Transformers [15], GShard [16], ST-MoE [17], V-MoE [18]. Claims Meta-Brain's routing is \"orthogonal and complementary.\"\n4. **Parameter-Efficient Adaptation**: LoRA [19], AdaLoRA [20], DoRA [21], LoRA-SP [22], MoIRA [23], CORAL [24]. Claims routing by perturbation type (from activations) rather than task identity.\n\nTotal: 24 references.\n\n### Core Claims\n1. Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-token granularity → zero-overhead diagnosis\n2. Perturbation-diagnostic routing to specialized LoRA experts outperforms parameter-matched universal LoRA by 13.8pp\n3. This is a general principle (cross-backbone validation, rank-invariant benefit)\n\n### Method\n- Phase 0: KNN probing on frozen layer 6 first-token activations → F1=1.0 (3-class)\n- PAR: 2-layer MLP on h^(6)[0] → softmax → routing weights\n- ALB: 3 LoRA experts (rank-16, Q/K/V/O on all 18 PaliGemma layers)\n- Soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k p_k · ΔW_k · h_frozen\n- Two-stage training: (1) experts independently on matched type, (2) freeze experts, train PAR end-to-end\n\n### Key Results\n- Meta-Brain 78.9% vs Universal r48 65.1% (+13.8pp, p<0.001)\n- Random routing 65.8% (n.s. over universal) → rules out ensemble\n- Oracle 80.7% (gap 1.8pp)\n- Cross-type drop 17.4pp avg\n- SmolVLA: +14.2pp routing benefit\n- Clean LIBERO: +0.3pp (preserved)\n- Rank sweep: benefit monotonically increasing (13.8→14.2pp)\n- Layer placement: V→early, S→mid, P→late\n\n### Experiment Coverage\nThe paper includes 12 tables covering: ablation ladder, statistical significance, per-perturbation breakdown, cross-type matrix, FTM comparison, rank sweep, parameter efficiency, clean LIBERO, cross-backbone, router architecture, routing granularity, layer placement. Plus correction memory ablation in text.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is the core idea? Is \"perturbation-diagnostic routing\" genuinely new, or is it a straightforward combination of known components (probing + MoE routing + LoRA)? How does it relate to existing MoE and task-conditioned LoRA work?\n2. **Related Work Completeness**: Are there missing references? Is the positioning fair? Is VLA-GSE fairly compared (different backbone)? Are there other relevant works in robustness, domain adaptation, test-time adaptation, or condition-specific routing that should be discussed?\n3. **Claim Validity**: Are the three stated contributions truly novel and supported by evidence?\n4. **Conceptual Concerns**: Is the closed-set 3-class taxonomy a fundamental limitation? How would this work with unknown perturbation types? Is the \"perturbation type is encoded in activations\" finding surprising or expected?\n5. **Strengths** (bullet list)\n6. **Weaknesses** (bullet list)\n7. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n8. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n9. **Scores**:\n   - Novelty (1-4): How novel are the ideas and results?\n   - Significance (1-4): How significant are the contributions?\n   - Relevance to Robot Learning (1-5): How relevant to CoRL?\n   - Overall Score (1-10): Using CoRL scale\n   - Confidence (1-5): How confident are you?\n\nBe critical but fair. Consider:\n- Whether the Phase 0 finding (activations encode perturbation type) is truly surprising — isn't it expected that different perturbations produce different activation patterns?\n- Whether \"diagnose then correct\" is conceptually novel vs. being a natural application of MoE with a domain-specific routing signal\n- Whether the 3-class closed-set assumption severely limits real-world applicability for a robotics venue\n- Whether the comparison to VLA-GSE (different backbone) is fair or misleading\n- Whether simulation-only evaluation is sufficient for CoRL\n- The relationship to test-time adaptation (TTA) literature which also routes/adapts based on input characteristics\n- Whether MoIRA [23] and CORAL [24] are sufficiently differentiated — they also do LoRA routing\n\nOutput your full review in structured markdown format.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":759,"cache_read_input_tokens":64399,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":759},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d09a97eb-2874-493a-b7bd-2546bcb68a45","uuid":"a67a4e32-8ffd-4c46-939d-498408f9c2a8"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a55c9af9ceac1df3d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LsBEg2hEM2UhXf8DbuTHmK","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Score scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 9=top 15% strong accept, 8=top 50% clear accept, 7=good accept, 6=marginally above threshold, 5=marginally below threshold, 4=rejection, 3=clear rejection, 2=strong rejection, 1=trivial/wrong)\n- Acceptance rate ~30%. Papers must be relevant to robot learning.\n\n## Paper: \"Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Robust Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n### Summary\nMeta-Brain diagnoses perturbation type from frozen VLA activations and routes to type-specialized LoRA experts. Key empirical finding: frozen PaliGemma layer 6 first-token activations achieve F1=1.0 on 3-class perturbation classification (visual/spatial/physical). A PAR router (2-layer MLP, 0.53M params) produces soft routing weights over 3 LoRA experts (LoRA-V, LoRA-S, LoRA-P, each rank-16, ~3.7M params). On LIBERO-Plus: Meta-Brain 78.9% vs parameter-matched universal LoRA r48 65.1% (+13.8pp, p<0.001). Random routing 65.8% (+0.7pp n.s.). Oracle gap 1.8pp. Cross-backbone on SmolVLA: +14.2pp. Clean LIBERO preserved.\n\n### Related Work Section (Section 2)\nThe paper covers 4 areas:\n1. **VLA Foundations**: pi0 [1], OpenVLA [2], SmolVLA [3], Octo [4], RT-2 [5]. Claims shared vulnerability to environmental perturbations.\n2. **VLA Robustness**: VLA-GSE [8] (MoE gating, 114M params, 81.2% on LIBERO-Plus with Qwen3-VL-4B), SAFE [9] (detection only, no correction), RobustVLA [10] (adversarial training), FTM [11] and FLA [12] (visual-only), SRPO [13] and CorridorVLA [14] (full model training). Claims \"None combines perturbation diagnosis with type-specific correction routing.\"\n3. **MoE Routing**: Switch Transformers [15], GShard [16], ST-MoE [17], V-MoE [18]. Claims Meta-Brain's routing is \"orthogonal and complementary.\"\n4. **Parameter-Efficient Adaptation**: LoRA [19], AdaLoRA [20], DoRA [21], LoRA-SP [22], MoIRA [23], CORAL [24]. Claims routing by perturbation type (from activations) rather than task identity.\n\nTotal: 24 references.\n\n### Core Claims\n1. Frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-token granularity → zero-overhead diagnosis\n2. Perturbation-diagnostic routing to specialized LoRA experts outperforms parameter-matched universal LoRA by 13.8pp\n3. This is a general principle (cross-backbone validation, rank-invariant benefit)\n\n### Method\n- Phase 0: KNN probing on frozen layer 6 first-token activations → F1=1.0 (3-class)\n- PAR: 2-layer MLP on h^(6)[0] → softmax → routing weights\n- ALB: 3 LoRA experts (rank-16, Q/K/V/O on all 18 PaliGemma layers)\n- Soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k p_k · ΔW_k · h_frozen\n- Two-stage training: (1) experts independently on matched type, (2) freeze experts, train PAR end-to-end\n\n### Key Results\n- Meta-Brain 78.9% vs Universal r48 65.1% (+13.8pp, p<0.001)\n- Random routing 65.8% (n.s. over universal) → rules out ensemble\n- Oracle 80.7% (gap 1.8pp)\n- Cross-type drop 17.4pp avg\n- SmolVLA: +14.2pp routing benefit\n- Clean LIBERO: +0.3pp (preserved)\n- Rank sweep: benefit monotonically increasing (13.8→14.2pp)\n- Layer placement: V→early, S→mid, P→late\n\n### Experiment Coverage\nThe paper includes 12 tables covering: ablation ladder, statistical significance, per-perturbation breakdown, cross-type matrix, FTM comparison, rank sweep, parameter efficiency, clean LIBERO, cross-backbone, router architecture, routing granularity, layer placement. Plus correction memory ablation in text.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is the core idea? Is \"perturbation-diagnostic routing\" genuinely new, or is it a straightforward combination of known components (probing + MoE routing + LoRA)? How does it relate to existing MoE and task-conditioned LoRA work?\n2. **Related Work Completeness**: Are there missing references? Is the positioning fair? Is VLA-GSE fairly compared (different backbone)? Are there other relevant works in robustness, domain adaptation, test-time adaptation, or condition-specific routing that should be discussed?\n3. **Claim Validity**: Are the three stated contributions truly novel and supported by evidence?\n4. **Conceptual Concerns**: Is the closed-set 3-class taxonomy a fundamental limitation? How would this work with unknown perturbation types? Is the \"perturbation type is encoded in activations\" finding surprising or expected?\n5. **Strengths** (bullet list)\n6. **Weaknesses** (bullet list)\n7. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n8. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n9. **Scores**:\n   - Novelty (1-4): How novel are the ideas and results?\n   - Significance (1-4): How significant are the contributions?\n   - Relevance to Robot Learning (1-5): How relevant to CoRL?\n   - Overall Score (1-10): Using CoRL scale\n   - Confidence (1-5): How confident are you?\n\nBe critical but fair. Consider:\n- Whether the Phase 0 finding (activations encode perturbation type) is truly surprising — isn't it expected that different perturbations produce different activation patterns?\n- Whether \"diagnose then correct\" is conceptually novel vs. being a natural application of MoE with a domain-specific routing signal\n- Whether the 3-class closed-set assumption severely limits real-world applicability for a robotics venue\n- Whether the comparison to VLA-GSE (different backbone) is fair or misleading\n- Whether simulation-only evaluation is sufficient for CoRL\n- The relationship to test-time adaptation (TTA) literature which also routes/adapts based on input characteristics\n- Whether MoIRA [23] and CORAL [24] are sufficiently differentiated — they also do LoRA routing\n\nOutput your full review in structured markdown format.\n","uuid":"e4b589de-f82f-42c6-a268-73b441db8ea4","session_id":"d09a97eb-2874-493a-b7bd-2546bcb68a45"}
